{
  "description": "Issue #1025 Part B (decline B1 from #1021): a parenthesized group headed by a callable with a GLUED selector reads exactly like its bare spelling. Measured at `f3066730`, before the fix, these two lines DISAGREED: `print pair[1]` printed 10, while `print ( pair[1] )` raised `ol-too-many-inputs` -- the group committed to `parenthesized-call` (`spec/grammar.md:215`) on the head token alone and read `[1]` as a one-element list ARGUMENT. That is the issue #1021 defect class exactly, and its sharpest form: the bare spelling WORKED and the parenthesized one failed, when adding parentheses to group an expression more explicitly is what a learner is taught to do. `parser.ts`'s branch comment claimed \"the grammar makes that lookahead total\"; it was blind to `postfix-expression` (`spec/grammar.md:192`), the other thing the grammar lets follow a complete `primary`. The two `print` events below carry the SAME value from the two spellings, which is the whole assertion -- a fixture that only checked for the absence of a diagnostic would also pass if the two spellings read as two different clean programs. Data profile because a postfix selector is a Data form (`scripts/profile-detection.mjs`), not because the list itself is. The spaced sibling `( pair [1] )` deliberately still MEANS the call -- adjacency is the discriminator, exactly as it already is for `-1` versus `- 1` -- and is pinned as a unit test in `packages/parser/src/multiline-expressions.test.mjs`.",
